orm{(\I-\M_S)\Y}^2=\sigma^2(n-p)+ oldsymbol{\updelta}\T(\I-\M_S) oldsymbol{\updelta}, where \M_S projects onto \C(\mathbf{S}) and (\I-\M_S)\mathbf{S} oldsymbol{\uptheta}= zero. The last term is nonnegative. Unlike random error, fixed rounding error does not inflate the variance of the estimator. It only biases it, and the bias is small when rounding is fine compared with the spread of the regressors.

The classical measurement error model This section sets up the two versions of the classical model, shows that its normal version cannot identify the slope from the observed data alone, and separates classical error from Berkson error, which does no harm to a linear regression. Structural and functional models In a structural model the true values are random draws, as in @thm-eiv-attenuation. In a functional model they are fixed unknown constants, one nuisance parameter per observation, which cannot be conditioned on because they are not observed. Attenuation is the same in both. [Attenuation with fixed true values] Let \mathbf{t}_1,\mathbf{t}_2,\dots\in\Real^p be fixed vectors with first coordinate 1, such that n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf{t}_i\mathbf{t}_i\T	o\A, a positive definite matrix. Let y_i=\mathbf{t}_i\T oldsymbol{\uptheta}+\varepsilon_i and \mathbf{s}_i=\mathbf{t}_i+	ilde{ u}_i, where the pairs (	ilde{ u}_i,\varepsilon_i) are independent and identically distributed, 	ilde{ u}_i and \varepsilon_i are independent with mean zero and finite variances, the first coordinate of 	ilde{ u}_i is zero (so that \mathbf{s}_i keeps the constant and 	ilde{ Sigma} has zero first row and column), and \Cov(	ilde{ u}_i)=	ilde{ Sigma}. Then the least squares coefficient vector from regressing y on \mathbf{s} converges in probability to (\A+	ilde{ Sigma})^{-1}\A oldsymbol{\uptheta}. Stack the vectors as rows of \mathbf{T}, \mathbf{S} and 	ilde{ U}. Then 	frac1n\mathbf{S}\T\mathbf{S}=	frac1n\mathbf{T}\T\mathbf{T}+	frac1n igl(\mathbf{T}\T	ilde{ U}+	ilde{ U}\T\mathbf{T} igr)+	frac1n	ilde{ U}\T	ilde{ U} . The last term tends to 	ilde{ Sigma} in probability by the weak law of large numbers. An entry n^{-1}\sum_it_{ij}	ilde{u}_{ik} of n^{-1}\mathbf{T}\T	ilde{ U} has mean zero and variance 	ilde{\Sigma}_{kk}\,n^{-2}\sum_it_{ij}^2	o0, so it tends to zero by Chebyshev’s inequality. Hence n^{-1}\mathbf{S}\T\mathbf{S}	o\A+	ilde{ Sigma}, which is positive definite. In the same way, n^{-1}\mathbf{S}\T\y=n^{-1}\mathbf{S}\T\mathbf{T} oldsymbol{\uptheta}+n^{-1}\mathbf{T}\T e+n^{-1}	ilde{ U}\T e. Here n^{-1}\mathbf{S}\T\mathbf{T}	o\A by the argument just given, n^{-1}\mathbf{T}\T e	o zero by Chebyshev’s inequality, and n^{-1}	ilde{ U}\T e	o zero by the weak law, since \E(	ilde{ u}_i\varepsilon_i)= zero by independence. Matrix inversion is continuous at a nonsingular matrix, which finishes the proof. For a straight line the slope tends to  eta s_x^2/(s_x^2+\sigma_u^2), where s_x^2 is the limiting variance of the fixed values: what matters is the spread of the true values relative to the error. Because the number of parameters grows with n, maximum likelihood can fail in the functional model (@exm-opt-neyman-scott, @exr-eiv-functional-ml). What the data cannot tell you In the normal structural model, X\sim\Normal(\mu_x,\sigma_x^2), U\sim\Normal(0,\sigma_u^2) and \varepsilon\sim\Normal(0,\sigma^2) are independent, W=X+U and Y=\alpha+ eta X+\varepsilon. Then (W,Y) is bivariate normal (@thm-mvn-linear) with mean (\mu_x,\alpha+ eta\mu_x) and covariance matrix  egin{pmatrix}\sigma_{ww}&\sigma_{wy}\\sigma_{wy}&\sigma_{yy}\end{pmatrix} = egin{pmatrix}\sigma_x^2+\sigma_u^2& eta\sigma_x^2\ eta\sigma_x^2& eta^2\sigma_x^2+\sigma^2\end{pmatrix}. {#eq-eiv-normal-structural} A bivariate normal distribution is fixed by five numbers, but the model has six parameters (\mu_x,\alpha, eta,\sigma_x^2,\sigma_u^2,\sigma^2), so data on (W,Y) cannot determine all six. [The slope is identified only up to an interval] Let (W,Y) be bivariate normal with covariance matrix entries \sigma_{ww},\sigma_{wy},\sigma_{yy}, where \sigma_{ww}\sigma_{yy}>\sigma_{wy}^2. If \sigma_{wy}
e0, a number b is the slope of some normal structural model with \sigma_x^2>0, \sigma_u^2\ge0 and \sigma^2\ge0 that produces this distribution iff b lies in the closed interval with end points b_1= rac{\sigma_{wy}}{\sigma_{ww}},\qquad b_2= rac{\sigma_{yy}}{\sigma_{wy}} . If \sigma_{wy}=0, the only such slope is b=0. A normal distribution is determined by its mean and covariance matrix, so a model reproduces the distribution iff it reproduces both. The means can always be matched by \mu_x=\E W and \alpha=\E Y-b\mu_x. By @eq-eiv-normal-structural the covariances are matched iff \sigma_x^2+\sigma_u^2=\sigma_{ww},\qquad b\,\sigma_x^2=\sigma_{wy},\qquad b^2\sigma_x^2+\sigma^2=\sigma_{yy}. If \sigma_{wy}=0, the middle equation with \sigma_x^2>0 forces b=0, and then \sigma_x^2\in(0,\sigma_{ww}] and \sigma^2=\sigma_{yy} complete a model. If \sigma_{wy}
e0, then b
e0 and the equations force \sigma_x^2=\sigma_{wy}/b, \sigma_u^2=\sigma_{ww}-\sigma_{wy}/b and \sigma^2=\sigma_{yy}-b\sigma_{wy}. These are admissible iff b has the sign of \sigma_{wy} (so \sigma_x^2>0), \lvert bvert\ge\lvert\sigma_{wy}vert/\sigma_{ww}=\lvert b_1vert (so \sigma_u^2\ge0) and \lvert bvert\le\sigma_{yy}/\lvert\sigma_{wy}vert=\lvert b_2vert (so \sigma^2\ge0). Since b_1 and b_2 share the sign of \sigma_{wy}, these conditions say that b lies between b_1 and b_2. The interval is not empty, because \lvert b_1vert\le\lvert b_2vert is equivalent to \sigma_{wy}^2\le\sigma_{ww}\sigma_{yy}. The end point b_1 is the least squares slope of Y on W, correct if there is no measurement error. The end point b_2 is the inverted slope of W on Y, correct if there is no equation error. Their ratio is the squared correlation, so the interval is short only when the correlation is high. [The interval for the blood-pressure study] For the first reading in the running example, the sample covariances are s_{ww}=216.21, s_{wy}=10.786 and s_{yy}=1.889. The compatible slopes form the interval [0.0499,\,0.1752], and the squared correlation is 0.285. It contains the true 0.08, whose implied variances \sigma_x^2=134.8, \sigma_u^2=81.4 and \sigma^2=1.026 are close to the generating 144, 81 and 1, but the data cannot prefer them to those of any other slope in the interval (Figure 24.2.1). [Figure 24.2.1. (a) Direct and reverse regression lines and slopes in between (grey), each fitting a normal structural model exactly; dashed, the true slope. (b) The reliability b_1/b and squared correlation b/b_2 that slope b would require.] Figure 24.2.1. (a) Direct and reverse regression lines and slopes in between (grey), each fitting a normal structural model exactly; dashed, the true slope. (b) The reliability b_1/b and squared correlation b/b_2 that slope b would require. [(a) Direct and reverse regression lines and slopes in between (grey), each fitting a normal structural model exactly; dashed, the true slope. (b) The reliability b_1/b and squared correlation b/b_2 that slope b would require.] (a) Direct and reverse regression lines and slopes in between (grey), each fitting a normal structural model exactly; dashed, the true slope. (b) The reliability b_1/b and squared correlation b/b_2 that slope b would require. import numpy as np rng = np.random.default_rng(2401) n = 300 age = rng.normal(55, 10, n) x = 130 + 0.6 * (age - 55) + rng.normal(0, np.sqrt(108), n) # long-run blood pressure, sd 12 w = x[:, None] + rng.normal(0, 9, (n, 2)) # two clinic readings y = -4 + 0.08 * x + 0.0 * age + rng.normal(0, 1.0, n) # age has no effect given x S = np.cov(w[:, 0], y) # sample covariance matrix of (reading, outcome) s_ww, s_wy, s_yy = S[0, 0], S[0, 1], S[1, 1] b_direct = s_wy / s_ww # assumes no measurement error (sigma_u^2 = 0) b_reverse = s_yy / s_wy # assumes no equation error (sigma^2 = 0) print(f"slopes compatible with the data: [{b_direct:.4f}, {b_reverse:.4f}]") for b in [b_direct, 0.08, b_reverse]: # the variances each slope would imply sx2 = s_wy / b print(f"b = {b:.4f}: sigma_x^2 = {sx2:7.2f}, sigma_u^2 = {s_ww - sx2:7.2f}, " f"sigma^2 = {s_yy - b * s_wy:.4f}") The sign of  eta, whether  eta=0, and the lower bound \lvert b_1vert on \lvert etavert are identified. Any information that pins down one variance identifies everything: \sigma_u^2, \lambda, the ratio \sigma^2/\sigma_u^2, or a bound \lambda\ge\lambda_0 that narrows the interval (@exr-eiv-bound). The rest of the chapter is about where such information comes from. [Nonnormal true values] Reiersøl (1950) showed that when  eta
e0 and the errors are normal and independent of X, the slope is identified exactly when X is not normal; higher moments then carry the missing information. We do not prove this. Estimators based on higher moments are highly variable unless X is far from normal, and rarely replace external information. Berkson error Sometimes the truth scatters around the recorded value instead: a furnace set to w degrees, every resident of a district assigned the district monitor’s pollution level, a solution prepared at a nominal concentration. Then X=W+U,\qquad U 	ext{ independent of } W, which is Berkson error (Berkson 1950). The difference from classical error is which variable the error is independent of. It cannot be settled from data on (W,Y), only from how the recorded value was produced. The scalar case with fixed settings appeared in Section 19.5; the proposition below allows several regressors and random recorded values. [Berkson error in linear regression] Let Y=\alpha+ beta\T\x+\varepsilon and \x= w+ u, where  w is fixed or random and, given  w, the errors  u and \varepsilon are independent, with means zero, covariance matrix  Sigma_{uu} and variance \sigma^2, not depending on  w. Then, given  w, \E(Y\mid w)=\alpha+ beta\T w,\qquad \Var(Y\mid w)=\sigma^2+ beta\T Sigma_{uu} beta , and if  u and \varepsilon are normal, so is Y given  w. Independent observations of this kind therefore satisfy the linear model in the recorded  w, with the same coefficients and error variance \sigma^2+ beta\T Sigma_{uu} beta. Given  w, Y=\alpha+ beta\T w+( beta\T u+\varepsilon), and the composite error is a linear combination of the independent  u and \varepsilon (@thm-rv-linear). So least squares on the settings is unbiased, and under normality the t and F procedures are exact, with s^2 estimating the enlarged error variance. Nothing needs correcting. [Concentrations set on a dial] Five solutions are prepared at each nominal concentration 10,20,\dots,60 mg/L (n=30); the achieved concentration adds \Normal(0,4^2) error, and the response is 2+0.5x+\varepsilon, \varepsilon\sim\Normal(0,3^2). Regressing on the nominal concentrations in 20000 simulated experiments gives a mean slope of 0.4994 and a mean s^2 of 13.01, matching \sigma^2+ eta^2\sigma_u^2=9+0.25\cdot16=13. The nominal 95% t intervals for the slope covered 0.5 in a proportion 0.952 of the experiments. from scipy import stats rng_b = np.random.default_rng(2410) settings = np.repeat([10.0, 20.0, 30.0, 40.0, 50.0, 60.0], 5) # dial settings, n = 30 Xd = np.column_stack([np.ones(30), settings]) reps = 20_000 achieved = settings + rng_b.normal(0, 4, (reps, 30)) # x = w + u, u independent of w Y = 2 + 0.5 * achieved + rng_b.normal(0, 3, (reps, 30)) B = np.linalg.solve(Xd.T @ Xd, Xd.T @ Y.T) # least squares on the settings s2 = np.sum((Y.T - Xd @ B) ** 2, axis=0) / 28 se = np.sqrt(s2 * np.linalg.inv(Xd.T @ Xd)[1, 1]) covered = np.abs(B[1] - 0.5) <= stats.t.ppf(0.975, 28) * se print(f"mean slope {B[1].mean():.4f}, mean s^2 {s2.mean():.2f} (sigma^2 + beta^2 sigma_u^2 = 13), " f"coverage {covered.mean():.3f}") Berkson error is harmless only because the model is linear in \x. For nonlinear g, \E\{g(\x)\mid w\}
e g( w) in general (@exr-eiv-berkson-quadratic). Real measurements often mix Berkson and classical components, which must then be handled separately. Exercises A. Check your understanding [A1] Classify each regressor error as classical, Berkson or neither, and say whether it biases the slope: (a) weight from a noisy scale; (b) a prescribed dose, when patients take a random fraction of it with mean one; (c) income rounded down to the nearest 10,000. [A2] In @prp-eiv-nonidentified, suppose it is known only that the reliability is at least \lambda_0\in(0,1]. Show that the compatible slopes are those between b_1 and \min(\lvert b_2vert,\lvert b_1vert/\lambda_0) in magnitude, with the sign of b_1. Evaluate the interval for @exm-eiv-identified-set with \lambda_0=0.5. The implied reliability of slope b is \sigma_x^2/\sigma_{ww}=b_1/b, so \lambda\ge\lambda_0 iff \lvert bvert\le\lvert b_1vert/\lambda_0. Intersect with the interval of the proposition. For the example, b_1/\lambda_0=0.0499/0.5=0.0998<0.1752, so the interval shrinks to [0.0499,\,0.0998], which still contains 0.08. B. Practice [B1] Let Y= eta_0+ eta_1X+ eta_2X^2+ eta_3X^3+\varepsilon and X=W+U with Berkson error U\sim\Normal(0,\sigma_u^2) independent of W and \varepsilon. Compute \E(Y\mid W). Which coefficients of a cubic regression on W are biased, and by how much? What happens for a quadratic? With \E U=\E U^3=0 and \E U^2=\sigma_u^2, \E(X^2\mid W)=W^2+\sigma_u^2 and \E(X^3\mid W)=W^3+3W\sigma_u^2. So \E(Y\mid W)=( eta_0+ eta_2\sigma_u^2)+( eta_1+3 eta_3\sigma_u^2)W+ eta_2W^2+ eta_3W^3 . The cubic regression on W estimates the intercept with bias  eta_2\sigma_u^2 and the linear coefficient with bias 3 eta_3\sigma_u^2. The top two coefficients are unbiased. For a quadratic ( eta_3=0) only the intercept is biased. The conditional variance also changes with W, so the errors are heteroscedastic. [B2] In the normal structural model, show that the reciprocal of the least squares slope of W on Y converges to b_2= eta+\sigma^2/( eta\sigma_x^2), so that it overstates \lvert etavert exactly when there is equation error. Evaluate b_2 for the population of the running example and compare it with @exm-eiv-identified-set. C. Going deeper [C1] In the functional model w_i=x_i+u_i, y_i=\alpha+ eta x_i+\varepsilon_i, with u_i,\varepsilon_i independent \Normal(0,\sigma^2) (equal, unknown variances) and x_1,\dots,x_n unknown constants: 1. show that, for fixed (\alpha, eta), maximizing the likelihood over each x_i leaves d_i^2=(y_i-\alpha- eta w_i)^2/(1+ eta^2), the squared perpendicular distance from (w_i,y_i) to the line; 2. deduce that the maximum likelihood line is the orthogonal regression line of Section 24.3, and that \hat{\sigma}^2_{	ext{ML}}=(2n)^{-1}\sum_i\hat{d}_i^2; 3. show that \hat{\sigma}^2_{	ext{ML}}	o\sigma^2/2 in probability, assuming the line is estimated consistently. Compare @exm-opt-neyman-scott. (a) The log likelihood is -n\log(2\pi\sigma^2)-\sum_i\{(w_i-x_i)^2+(y_i-\alpha- eta x_i)^2\}/(2\sigma^2). For each i the bracket is the squared distance from (w_i,y_i) to the point (x_i,\alpha+ eta x_i) of the line, and its minimum over x_i is the squared perpendicular distance d_i^2. (b) The profile log likelihood is -n\log(2\pi\sigma^2)-\sum_id_i^2/(2\sigma^2). It is maximized over the line by minimizing \sum_id_i^2, which is orthogonal regression, and over \sigma^2 by \sum_i\hat{d}_i^2/(2n). (c) At the true line, y_i-\alpha- eta w_i=\varepsilon_i- eta u_i has variance \sigma^2(1+ eta^2), so n^{-1}\sum_id_i^2	o\sigma^2 by the law of large numbers. A consistent estimate of the line gives the same limit, so \hat{\sigma}^2_{	ext{ML}}	o\sigma^2/2: each nuisance x_i uses up half of its observation’s two degrees of freedom.

Reliability and moment correction This section supplies the missing information of Section 24.2 directly, as a reliability ratio, an error covariance or a variance ratio, and solves @thm-eiv-attenuation for  oldsymbol{\uptheta}. Correcting with a known reliability If \lambda is known, for instance from a test–retest correlation (@exr-eiv-reliability-correlation in Section 24.1), then \hat{ eta}/\lambda is consistent in simple regression. For correlations this is Spearman’s (1904) correction for attenuation, \operatorname{Corr}(X_1,X_2)=\operatorname{Corr}(W_1,W_2)/\sqrt{\lambda_1\lambda_2} (@exr-eiv-spearman). With covariates the right divisor is \lambda_{x\mid z} (@exr-eiv-conditional-reliability). Dividing by \lambda undercorrects and leaves the covariates’ bias untouched. A known error covariance: the corrected estimator Work with raw second moments, so the intercept needs no special treatment. Collect the true regressors, including the constant, in \mathbf{t}=(1,\x\T, z\T)\T\in\Real^p, and the observed ones in \mathbf{s}=\mathbf{t}+	ilde{ u}, where 	ilde{ u}=(0, u\T, zero\T)\T has zeros for the constant and for the exactly measured  z. Write the model as Y=\mathbf{t}\T oldsymbol{\uptheta}+\varepsilon. Under classical error, \E(\mathbf{s}\mathbf{s}\T)=\E(\mathbf{t}\mathbf{t}\T)+	ilde{ Sigma},\qquad \E(\mathbf{s}Y)=\E(\mathbf{t}\mathbf{t}\T) oldsymbol{\uptheta}, \qquad 	ilde{ Sigma}=\E(	ilde{ u}	ilde{ u}\T), since the cross terms vanish. So  oldsymbol{\uptheta}=\{\E(\mathbf{s}\mathbf{s}\T)-	ilde{ Sigma}\}^{-1}\E(\mathbf{s}Y): subtract the error’s share of the moment matrix before inverting. To cover a known 	ilde{ Sigma} and one estimated from replicates at once, let observation i carry a matrix  D_i, computed from its own data, with expectation 	ilde{ Sigma}. If 	ilde{ Sigma} is known,  D_i=	ilde{ Sigma}. [Method-of-moments correction] Let (\mathbf{t}_i,	ilde{ u}_i,\varepsilon_i, D_i), i=1,2,\dots, be independent copies of (\mathbf{t},	ilde{ u},\varepsilon, D), where \mathbf{s}_i=\mathbf{t}_i+	ilde{ u}_i and y_i=\mathbf{t}_i\T oldsymbol{\uptheta}+\varepsilon_i are observed, and  D_i is a symmetric p	imes p matrix computed from the data of observation i. Assume that \E(\varepsilon)=0, \E(\mathbf{t}\varepsilon)= zero, \E(	ilde{ u}\varepsilon)= zero, \E(\mathbf{t}	ilde{ u}\T)= zero, \E( D)=	ilde{ Sigma}=\E(	ilde{ u}	ilde{ u}\T), and that \A=\E(\mathbf{t}\mathbf{t}\T) is positive definite. Let \hat{\A}_n= rac1n\sum_{i=1}^n igl(\mathbf{s}_i\mathbf{s}_i\T- D_i igr),\qquad \hat{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}}_{	ext{mm}}=\hat{\A}_n^{-1}\, rac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf{s}_iy_i . {#eq-eiv-corrected} 1. With probability one, \hat{\A}_n is nonsingular for all large n and \hat{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}}_{	ext{mm}}	o oldsymbol{\uptheta}. 2. If \mathbf{t}, 	ilde{ u} and \varepsilon have finite fourth moments and  D has finite second moments, then \sqrt n(\hat{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}}_{	ext{mm}}- oldsymbol{\uptheta})	o\Normal_p igl( zero,\A^{-1}\B\A^{-1} igr) \quad	ext{in distribution}, where \B=\E( oldsymbol{\uppsi} oldsymbol{\uppsi}\T) and  oldsymbol{\uppsi}=\mathbf{s}(y-\mathbf{s}\T oldsymbol{\uptheta})+ D oldsymbol{\uptheta}. 3. Under the conditions of (b), \hat{\A}_n^{-1}\hat{\B}_n\hat{\A}_n^{-1}	o\A^{-1}\B\A^{-1} in probability, where \hat{\B}_n=n^{-1}\sum_i\hat{ oldsymbol{\uppsi}}_i\hat{ oldsymbol{\uppsi}}_i\T and \hat{ oldsymbol{\uppsi}}_i is  oldsymbol{\uppsi}_i with \hat{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}}_{	ext{mm}} in place of  oldsymbol{\uptheta}. (a) By the strong law of large numbers, n^{-1}\sum_i\mathbf{s}_i\mathbf{s}_i\T	o\E(\mathbf{s}\mathbf{s}\T)=\A+	ilde{ Sigma} (the cross terms vanish since \E(\mathbf{t}	ilde{ u}\T)= zero) and n^{-1}\sum_i D_i	o	ilde{ Sigma}. So \hat{\A}_n	o\A, which is nonsingular, and \hat{\A}_n is nonsingular for all large n because the determinant is continuous. Also n^{-1}\sum_i\mathbf{s}_iy_i	o\E\{(\mathbf{t}+	ilde{ u})(\mathbf{t}\T oldsymbol{\uptheta}+\varepsilon)\}=\A oldsymbol{\uptheta}. By continuity of the inverse, \hat{ oldsym