e zero. So the Hessian is negative definite everywhere on \mathcal B and \ell is strictly concave there. Concavity settles uniqueness but not existence: a strictly concave function on an open set need not attain its supremum. Both failures happen in practice. [Two ways the maximum fails to exist] All counts zero. For a Poisson log-linear model with an intercept, if every y_i=0 then \ell( beta)=-\sum_ie^{\x_{(i)}\T beta}, which increases to its supremum 0 as  eta_0	o-\infty and is never attained. Separated binary data. If there is a vector \mathbf{v} with \x_{(i)}\T\mathbf{v}>0 whenever y_i=1 and \x_{(i)}\T\mathbf{v}<0 whenever y_i=0, then along the ray t\mathbf{v} every fitted probability moves towards the observed response, so \ell increases without reaching a maximum. Chapter 35 proves that this is the only way the binary maximum likelihood estimate can fail to exist (@thm-bin-separation). The general statement belongs to exponential-family theory. Under a canonical link  T=\X\T\diag( w)\y is the sufficient statistic of Section 34.1, and by @cor-glm-marginals the likelihood equations say \X\T\diag( w)\hat{ mu}= T. The attainable mean-value vectors \{\X\T\diag( w) mu:\  mu\in\mathcal M^n\} form an open convex set, and the maximum likelihood estimate exists precisely when  T lies inside it rather than on its boundary (Barndorff-Nielsen 1978, chapter 9; not reproduced here). Both examples above are boundary cases: all-zero counts put  T at the corner  zero, separation puts it on a face. For non-canonical links even uniqueness can fail. Concavity is a property of the canonical link, not of generalized linear models. Take the gamma family with the identity link, where \ell=\phi^{-1}\sum_i\{-y_i/\mu_i-\log\mu_i\} with \mu_i=\x_{(i)}\T beta. The second derivative of one term with respect to \mu_i is (\mu_i-2y_i)/\mu_i^3, positive whenever \mu_i>2y_i, so the Hessian is a difference of nonnegative definite matrices and can be indefinite: an iteration can stop at a local ma