The EM algorithm Maximizing the observed-data likelihood @eq-mis-observed-likelihood directly means differentiating an integral. EM avoids that: it alternates between filling in the missing pieces of the complete-data log-likelihood and maximizing the filled-in version, and it never decreases the observed-data likelihood. The iteration of @thm-dsn-missing(c) — fill each lost plot with its fitted value, refit, repeat — is the oldest instance in this book. The two steps Let \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta})=\log f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}) and \ell_{\mathrm{c}}( oldsymbol{\uptheta})=\log f(\mathbf{D}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}) be the observed-data and complete-data log-likelihoods, the second a function of data we do not have. The mechanism is ignorable throughout, so @thm-mis-ignorable licenses working with \ell. [EM] Given a current value  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}, define Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) =\E igl\{\ell_{\mathrm{c}}( oldsymbol{\uptheta})\  ig|\ \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)} igr\}, {#eq-mis-expected-complete} the expectation being over \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}} under the conditional density f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}). The E step computes Q(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}); the M step sets  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)} to a maximizer of it. If the M step only increases Q, the scheme is called generalized EM. The E step is not “fill in the missing values”; it fills in whatever functions of them the complete-data log-likelihood contains. When \ell_{\mathrm{c}} is linear in a set of sufficient statistics, as for every exponential family (@def-glm-edf), the E step reduces to filling in those statistics (@exr-mis-em-exponential-family) — which is the same as filling in the missing values only when the statistics are linear in them. A normal variance, whose statistic contains \sum_iy_i^2, is the standing counterexample, and only the second reading is EM. Monotonicity [The EM sequence never decreases the likelihood] Let  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)} satisfy Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) \ge Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}). Then 1. \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)})\ge\ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}), with equality only if Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) =Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) and the two conditional densities of \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}} agree almost everywhere; 2. if \ell is bounded above, \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) converges; 3. if Q(\cdot\mid\cdot) is differentiable in its first argument and  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)} is an interior maximizer, then at any fixed point  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*} of the iteration the observed-data score vanishes: \partial\ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta})/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}  ig|_{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*}}= zero. Write g(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}) =f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}) for the conditional density of the missing part. Since f(\mathbf{D}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}) =f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta})\, g(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}), taking logarithms and rearranging gives, for every \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}, \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta})=\ell_{\mathrm{c}}( oldsymbol{\uptheta}) -\log g(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}). The left side does not depend on \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}, so taking the expectation of both sides under g(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) leaves it unchanged and yields the decomposition  egin{gathered} \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta})=Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) -H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}),\quad	ext{where}\ H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) =\E igl\{\log g(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta})\  ig|\ \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)} igr\}. \end{gathered} {#eq-mis-em-decomposition} (a) Subtract @eq-mis-em-decomposition at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)} from the same at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}:  egin{aligned} \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)})-\ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) &= igl\{Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) -Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) igr\}\ &\qquad- igl\{H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) -H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) igr\}. \end{aligned} The first brace is nonnegative by hypothesis. For the second, write g_s=g(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) and g_{s+1}=g(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}); then  egin{aligned} H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s+1)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) -H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}) &=\int g_s\log rac{g_{s+1}}{g_s}\ &\le\log\int g_s rac{g_{s+1}}{g_s}=\log 1=0 , \end{aligned} by Jensen’s inequality applied to the concave logarithm. So the difference of log-likelihoods is a nonnegative number minus a nonpositive one. Equality throughout forces equality in Jensen’s inequality, which for the strictly concave logarithm requires g_{s+1}/g_s to be constant almost everywhere under g_s, hence g_{s+1}=g_s, and forces the first brace to vanish. (b) A nondecreasing sequence bounded above converges. (c) At a fixed point,  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*} maximizes Q(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*}), so \partial Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*})/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta} vanishes at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}= oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*}. The function  oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mapsto H( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*}) is maximized at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}= oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*} by the Jensen argument just given, so its derivative vanishes there too. Differentiating @eq-mis-em-decomposition at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*} gives \partial\ell/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}= zero- zero. Part (c) says fixed points are stationary points, not maxima: EM can converge to a saddle point or a local maximum, and with several modes the limit depends on the start. Wu (1983) gives conditions under which the limit points are stationary and under which the sequence converges; none rules out a local maximum. Start from several values. Missing responses in a linear model The oldest case is the one where every step is explicit, and it settles a question left open in Section 18.6. [EM for a normal linear model with missing responses] Let \y=\X beta+ e with  e\sim\Normal_n( zero,\sigma^2\I) and ank(\X)=p; let the responses of a set of m rows be unrecorded, the covariates of those rows being known, with an ignorable mechanism, and suppose the observed rows \X_o have rank p. Write \mathrm{SSE}_o for the residual sum of squares of the least squares fit on the observed rows. Then: 1. the E step replaces each missing y_i by \x_{(i)}\T beta^{(s)} in the cross-product terms of \ell_{\mathrm{c}}, and each y_i^2 by (\x_{(i)}\T beta^{(s)})^2+\sigma^{2(s)}; 2. the M step is  beta^{(s+1)}=(\X\T\X)^{-1}\X\T\y^{(s)} with \y^{(s)} the completed vector, and \sigma^{2(s+1)}=n^{-1}\{
orm{\y^{(s)}-\X beta^{(s+1)}}^{2}+m\sigma^{2(s)}\}; 3. the unique fixed point is  beta^{*}=\hbeta_o, the least squares estimate from the observed rows, and \sigma^{2*}= rac{\mathrm{SSE}_o}{n-m}, {#eq-mis-em-sigma} which is the observed-data maximum likelihood estimate, based on n-m observations. (a) The complete-data log-likelihood -	frac12 n\log(2\pi\sigma^2)-	frac1{2\sigma^2}\sum_i(y_i-\x_{(i)}\T beta)^2 is quadratic in the y_i, so its conditional expectation needs their first two conditional moments. Given the observed data an unrecorded y_i is a draw from its own model, so those are \x_{(i)}\T beta^{(s)} and (\x_{(i)}\T beta^{(s)})^2+\sigma^{2(s)}. (b) With those substitutions, Q=-	frac12 n\log(2\pi\sigma^2)- rac1{2\sigma^2} \Bigl\{
