orm{\y_o-\X_o b}^2 and equals \hbeta_o, which is @thm-dsn-missing(a). Its residual sum of squares is \mathrm{SSE}_o, so the variance equation reads n\sigma^{2*}=\mathrm{SSE}_o+m\sigma^{2*}, giving @eq-mis-em-sigma. Uniqueness of  beta^{*} follows from @thm-dsn-missing(b), and @eq-mis-em-sigma then determines \sigma^{2*}. The term m\sigma^{2(s)} is the whole point. Without it — fill the gaps with fitted values and hand the completed data to a regression routine — the variance comes out as \mathrm{SSE}_o/n. That is the error @thm-dsn-missing(e) warns against, now derived rather than asserted: the filled-in values are not data, and the likelihood knows it. [The Mauna Loa gaps] Fit the trend-plus-harmonics model of Section 5.3 to all 2284 weeks, with the 59 missing ones handled by EM. The coefficients agree with the complete-case fit to machine precision, as @prp-mis-em-responses(c) requires, and the error standard deviation is 0.7992 ppm; filling in the gaps and treating them as data gives 0.7889 ppm, too small by the factor \sqrt{n/(n-m)}=1.0132. The discrepancy is small here only because the gaps are few. import numpy as np import statsmodels.api as sm co2 = sm.datasets.co2.load_pandas().data["co2"] t = (co2.index.year + (co2.index.dayofyear - 0.5) / 365.25).to_numpy() tc = t - 1980.0 X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(t)), tc, tc**2, np.cos(2 * np.pi * t), np.sin(2 * np.pi * t), np.cos(4 * np.pi * t), np.sin(4 * np.pi * t)]) y = co2.to_numpy() obs = ~np.isnan(y) n, m, p = len(y), int((~obs).sum()), X.shape[1] beta, sigma2 = np.zeros(p), 1.0 # any starting value will do for sweep in range(60): filled = np.where(obs, y, X @ beta) # E step: gaps get their fitted values beta, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(X, filled, rcond=None) # M step for the coefficients sse = float(np.sum((filled - X @ beta) ** 2)) sigma2 = (sse + m * sigma2) / n # M step for the variance: add m*sigma^2 print(f"EM after {sweep + 1} sweeps: sigma-hat = {np.sqrt(sigma2):.4f} ppm") beta_cc, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(X[obs], y[obs], rcond=None) sse_o = float(np.sum((y[obs] - X[obs] @ beta_cc) ** 2)) print(f"observed-data MLE: {np.sqrt(sse_o / (n - m)):.4f} ppm") print(f"treating the fills as data: {np.sqrt(sse_o / n):.4f} ppm") A missing covariate When a covariate is missing, the conditional distribution the E step needs runs backwards: what does a recorded response say about an unrecorded covariate? A model for the covariate is now unavoidable. [EM with a missing covariate] Let x_i\sim\Normal(\mu,	au^2) independently, and y_i\mid x_i\sim\Normal( eta_0+ eta_1x_i,\sigma^2), with  oldsymbol{\uptheta}=( eta_0, eta_1,\sigma^2,\mu,	au^2). Suppose y_i is always recorded and x_i is missing for a subset, with an ignorable mechanism. Then for a record with x_i missing the E step uses \E(x_i\mid y_i)=\mu+ rac{ eta_1	au^2}{ eta_1^2	au^2+\sigma^2}  igl(y_i- eta_0- eta_1\mu igr),\qquad \Var(x_i\mid y_i)=	au^2- rac{ eta_1^2	au^4}{ eta_1^2	au^2+\sigma^2}, {#eq-mis-em-estep} both evaluated at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}, and the M step is the complete-data maximum likelihood fit with the sufficient statistics \sum_ix_i, \sum_ix_i^2 and \sum_ix_iy_i replaced by \sum_i\E(x_i\mid y_i), \sum_i\E(x_i^2\mid y_i) and \sum_iy_i\E(x_i\mid y_i). The pair (x_i,y_i) is bivariate normal, with \E y_i= eta_0+ eta_1\mu, \Var y_i= eta_1^2	au^2+\sigma^2 and \Cov(x_i,y_i)= eta_1	au^2. The conditional moments @eq-mis-em-estep are the standard normal conditional mean and variance (@thm-mvn-conditional). The complete-data log-likelihood is -	frac{n}2\log\sigma^2- rac{\sum_i(y_i- eta_0- eta_1x_i)^2}{2\sigma^2} -	frac{n}2\log	au^2- rac{\sum_i(x_i-\mu)^2}{2	au^2}+	ext{const}, which is linear in the three listed statistics together with \sum_iy_i and \sum_iy_i^2; taking conditional expectations therefore replaces exactly those statistics, and the maximizer is the complete-data one evaluated at them. Note what the E step does not do: it does not impute x_i by \mu, nor from other covariates alone. It uses the response. Imputing a covariate without the response is among the commonest errors in practice, and it attenuates \hat eta_1 towards zero, for the reason Chapter 24 gives. [An EM fit with a missing covariate] Simulate N=400 records with  eta_1=0.8, and let the covariate be missing more often when the response is large; this loses 169 covariate values. The mechanism is MAR — it uses only the recorded y — so likelihood is valid and the complete-case fit is not. EM gives \hat eta_1=0.825; the complete cases give 0.620, attenuated by a quarter. Figure 41.4.1 shows the two faces of the algorithm. The observed-data log-likelihood climbs monotonically, as @thm-mis-em(a) requires, and the distance to the fixed point falls by the constant factor 0.760 per sweep, matching to three decimals the largest eigenvalue of \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{m}} below. Reaching eight decimal places takes 68 sweeps — slow by the standards of Newton’s method, which is the price of never differentiating an integral. [Figure 41.4.1. EM for a linear model with a missing covariate. (a) The observed-data log-likelihood increases at every sweep. (b) The distance from the current iterate to the fixed point decays geometrically at the rate predicted by the fraction of missing information.] Figure 41.4.1. EM for a linear model with a missing covariate. (a) The observed-data log-likelihood increases at every sweep. (b) The distance from the current iterate to the fixed point decays geometrically at the rate predicted by the fraction of missing information. [EM for a linear model with a missing covariate. (a) The observed-data log-likelihood increases at every sweep. (b) The distance from the current iterate to the fixed point decays geometrically at the rate predicted by the fraction of missing information.] EM for a linear model with a missing covariate. (a) The observed-data log-likelihood increases at every sweep. (b) The distance from the current iterate to the fixed point decays geometrically at the rate predicted by the fraction of missing information. rng = np.random.default_rng(4104) N = 400 theta_true = dict(b0=1.0, b1=0.8, s2=1.0, mu=0.5, t2=1.0) x = theta_true["mu"] + np.sqrt(theta_true["t2"]) * rng.normal(size=N) y2 = theta_true["b0"] + theta_true["b1"] * x + np.sqrt(theta_true["s2"]) * rng.normal(size=N) seen = rng.uniform(size=N) > 1 / (1 + np.exp(-(-0.4 + 2.0 * (y2 - y2.mean())))) print(f"{N - seen.sum()} of {N} covariates missing, more often when y is large") def estep(th, y, seen): """Conditional mean and second moment of the covariate given the response.""" b0, b1, s2, mu, t2 = th vy = b1**2 * t2 + s2 # marginal variance of y cond_mean = mu + (b1 * t2 / vy) * (y - b0 - b1 * mu) cond_var = t2 - (b1 * t2) ** 2 / vy ex = np.where(seen, x, cond_mean) ex2 = np.where(seen, x**2, cond_mean**2 + cond_var) return ex, ex2 def mstep(ex, ex2, y): mu, n_ = ex.mean(), len(y) t2 = ex2.mean() - mu**2 sxx = ex2.sum() - n_ * mu**2 sxy = (ex * y).sum() - n_ * mu * y.mean() b1 = sxy / sxx b0 = y.mean() - b1 * mu s2 = ((y**2).sum() - 2 * b0 * y.sum() - 2 * b1 * (ex * y).sum() + n_ * b0**2 + 2 * b0 * b1 * ex.sum() + b1**2 * ex2.sum()) / n_ return np.array([b0, b1, s2, mu, t2]) def loglik(th, y, seen): """Observed-data log-likelihood: joint for complete rows, marginal for the rest.""" b0, b1, s2, mu, t2 = th ll = -0.5 * np.sum(np.log(2 * np.pi * s2) + (y[seen] - b0 - b1 * x[seen]) ** 2 / s2) ll += -0.5 * np.sum(np.log(2 * np.pi * t2) + (x[seen] - mu) ** 2 / t2) vy = b1**2 * t2 + s2 ll += -0.5 * np.sum(np.log(2 * np.pi * vy) + (y[~seen] - b0 - b1 * mu) ** 2 / vy) return ll th = np.array([0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0]) path, iterates = [loglik(th, y2, seen)], [th] for sweep in range(200): ex, ex2 = estep(th, y2, seen) th = mstep(ex, ex2, y2) path.append(loglik(th, y2, seen)) iterates.append(th) print("EM b0, b1 =", np.round(th[:2], 4)) cc = np.polyfit(x[seen], y2[seen], 1)[::-1] # complete cases only print("complete cases:", np.round(cc, 4)) Rate of convergence, and the standard errors EM does not give Both the speed of EM and its principal defect come from the same matrix. Define the complete-data information \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}=-\partial^2Q( oldsymbol{\uptheta}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*})/ \partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\,\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T at  oldsymbol{\uptheta}= oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{*}, the observed information \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{o}}=-\partial^2\ell/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\,\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T there, and their difference \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{m}}=\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}-\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{o}}, the missing information. [The missing-information principle] At any  oldsymbol{\uptheta}, with  S_{\mathrm{c}} the complete-data score,  rac{\partial\ell}{\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}} =\E igl( S_{\mathrm{c}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta} igr), \qquad \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{o}} =\E igl(-\partial S_{\mathrm{c}}/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T \mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta} igr) -\Var igl( S_{\mathrm{c}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta} igr). {#eq-mis-louis} Consequently \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{m}} =\Var( S_{\mathrm{c}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}) is nonnegative definite: the observed information is never larger than the expected complete-data information. Differentiate the identity \ell( oldsymbol{\uptheta})=\ell_{\mathrm{c}}( oldsymbol{\uptheta}) -\log g(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}) from the proof of @thm-mis-em and take the conditional expectation under g(\cdot\mid oldsymbol{\uptheta}). The conditional score of g has mean zero, being the score of a density in its own parameter, so the first identity follows. Differentiating again, - rac{\partial^2\ell}{\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T} =- rac{\partial^2\ell_{\mathrm{c}}}{\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T} + rac{\partial^2\log g}{\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T}, and taking the conditional expectation turns the last term into minus the information of g, which by the usual information identity equals minus the variance of its score. Since the score of g is  S_{\mathrm{c}}-\partial\ell/\partial oldsymbol{\uptheta} and the second term is a constant given the observed data, that variance is \Var( S_{\mathrm{c}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}). Identity @eq-mis-louis is Louis’s formula (Louis 1982); “observed = complete minus missing” is the missing-information principle of Orchard and Woodbury (1972). Two consequences: The rate. Near a fixed point the EM map is approximately linear with Jacobian \I-\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{o}} =\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{m}}, so the iterates converge linearly at its largest eigenvalue — the largest fraction of missing information (Dempster, Laird and Rubin, 1977). In @exm-mis-em-covariate that eigenvalue is 0.760 and the measured rate is 0.760; the log-likelihood gap, quadratic near the maximum, falls at its square, 0.577 per sweep. In @thm-dsn-missing(c) the same matrix is \M_{mm}, the submatrix of the hat matrix belonging to the lost plots. The standard errors. The matrix EM manipulates is \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{c}}, which is too large, so inverting it understates the variance by the fraction of missing information. Three honest routes: @eq-mis-louis; numerical differentiation of \ell, as this section’s script does; or the bootstrap, resampling records and rerunning EM (@def-bs-bootstrap(b)). In @exm-mis-em-covariate these give 0.0611 and 0.0605 (bootstrap, 300 resamples), against 0.0512 from the complete-data information — an understatement of 16\%. The variance of an EM estimate is never the inverse of the matrix the M step inverts. If a routine reports standard errors after EM without saying how it got them, assume it has made this mistake. Exercises A. Check your understanding [A1] The EM algorithm for the zero-inflated model of Section 37.5 treats the component label as the missing datum. Identify \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}, Q and the M step there, and check that @exr-cnt-em-monotone is the special case of @thm-mis-em(a). B. Practice [B1] In @prp-mis-em-responses, suppose one carries out the iteration but omits the term m\sigma^{2(s)} from the M step for the variance. Show that the coefficients are unaffected and that the variance converges to \mathrm{SSE}_o/n. Relate the discrepancy to @thm-dsn-missing(e). The coefficient update does not involve \sigma^2 at all, so  beta^{(s)} follows the same sequence and converges to \hbeta_o; at that point the completed residual sum of squares is \mathrm{SSE}_o. The truncated variance update is \sigma^{2(s+1)}=\mathrm{SSE}_o/n once the coefficients have settled. That is the complete-data formula applied to n “observations”, m of which have been manufactured to fit exactly; reducing the divisor by m is the degrees-of-freedom correction of @thm-dsn-missing(e). [B2] Let the complete-data model be an exponential dispersion family (@def-glm-edf) with sufficient statistic \mathbf{t}(\mathbf{D}). Show that the E step reduces to computing \E\{\mathbf{t}(\mathbf{D})\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\uptheta}^{(s)}\} and the M step to solving the complete-data likelihood equations with \mathbf{t} replaced by that expectation. Why does the normal variance in @prp-mis-em-responses still require the extra term m\sigma^{2(s)}? The complete-data log-likelihood is  oldsymbol{\uptheta}\T\mathbf{t}(\mathbf{D})-A( oldsymbol{\uptheta}) plus a term free of  oldsymbol{\uptheta}, so it is affine in \mathbf{t} and its conditional expectation replaces \mathbf{t} by \E(\mathbf{t}\mid\cdot). Setting the derivative to zero gives \E_{ oldsymbol{\uptheta}}\mathbf{t}=\E(\mathbf{t}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}). The normal case is no exception: the sufficient statistics include \sum_iy_i^2, and its conditional expectation over a missing row is (\x_{(i)}\T beta^{(s)})^2+\sigma^{2(s)}, which is where the extra term comes from. The trap is to substitute the conditional mean of the data rather than the conditional mean of the statistic. C. Going deeper [C1] Construct a two-component normal mixture with missing labels — the classical EM problem — for which the likelihood is unbounded, and show that EM started near a degenerate configuration drives one variance to zero while the likelihood increases at every step. Reconcile this with @thm-mis-em(b).

Multiple imputation Filling a hole with a plausible value is not wrong; filling it with one plausible value and then forgetting that it was a guess is. Multiple imputation keeps the convenience of a complete rectangular data set and restores the uncertainty a single filling destroys, by filling several times and letting the variation between fillings speak. The idea Rubin’s proposal (1978, 1987) has three parts. 1. Impute. Draw M independent completions \mathbf{D}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{D}^{(M)}, each replacing \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}} by a draw from its posterior predictive distribution given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}} under an imputation model. 2. Analyse. Apply the intended complete-data analysis to each completion, getting estimates \hat Q_1,\dots,\hat Q_M and complete-data variance estimates U_1,\dots,U_M. 3. Combine. Pool them by @thm-mis-rubin. Step 1 is the only novelty. Drawing from the posterior predictive distribution means drawing the imputation model’s parameters as well as the values; an imputation that conditions on a point estimate treats it as known, which is one of the ways the procedure fails. Multiple imputation Let  oldsymbol{\upgamma} parameterize a model for the conditional distribution of \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}} given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, with prior p( oldsymbol{\upgamma}). An imputation is proper in Rubin’s sense if the M completions are independent draws from f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}) =\int f(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{mis}}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, oldsymbol{\upgamma})\, p( oldsymbol{\upgamma}\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})\,d oldsymbol{\upgamma}, {#eq-mis-posterior-predictive} the posterior predictive distribution. Three standard constructions: 1. Regression imputation with a parameter draw. For a continuous variable modelled as normal given the others, draw \sigma^{2*}=\mathrm{SSE}/\chi^2_{k-q} from its posterior under a flat prior, then  oldsymbol{\upgamma}^{*}\sim\Normal(\hat{ oldsymbol{\upgamma}}, \sigma^{2*}(\Z_o\T\Z_o)^{-1}), then impute \mathbf{z}\T oldsymbol{\upgamma}^{*}+\sigma^{*}\varepsilon with \varepsilon standard normal. This is @thm-opt-bayes-conjugate with the noninformative limit @cor-opt-flat-prior. 2. Predictive mean matching. Compute the predicted value for the record with the gap, find the d recorded records with the closest predicted values, and impute one of their observed values at random. Every imputed value then actually occurred, so the marginal distribution is respected even when the normal model is wrong, at the cost of a less transparent theory. 3. Chained equations. With gaps in several variables, cycle through them, imputing each in turn from a regression on the current versions of all the others, for several sweeps, and keep the completion at the end of a sweep. Construction (c), also called fully conditional specification or MICE (van Buuren, 2018), is what makes multiple imputation usable on real files, where dozens of variables have gaps and no one will write a joint density for all of them. It is a Gibbs-like sweep (compare @exm-mix-gibbs) carrying a risk the Gibbs sampler does not: the specified conditionals need not correspond to any joint distribution, and then the sweep has no stationary distribution and the result depends on the order of the variables. The dependence is usually slight, and the flexibility is worth the untidiness, but it should be known. Rubin’s rules [Combining M analyses] Let Q be the estimand and suppose the complete-data analysis is calibrated in the sense that, given the complete data \mathbf{D}, it reports \hat Q(\mathbf{D}) and U(\mathbf{D}) with \E(Q\mid\mathbf{D})=\hat Q(\mathbf{D}),\qquad \Var(Q\mid\mathbf{D})=U(\mathbf{D}). {#eq-mis-complete-calibration} Let \mathbf{D}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{D}^{(M)} be independent draws from @eq-mis-posterior-predictive, generated by the analyst and therefore conditionally independent of Q given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}; put \hat Q_\ell=\hat Q(\mathbf{D}^{(\ell)}) and U_\ell=U(\mathbf{D}^{(\ell)}), and define  ar Q= rac1M\sum_{\ell}\hat Q_\ell,\qquad  ar U= rac1M\sum_{\ell}U_\ell,\qquad B= rac1{M-1}\sum_{\ell}(\hat Q_\ell- ar Q)^2 . {#eq-mis-rubin-pieces} Then, with all expectations conditional on \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}: 1. (Decomposition.) \Var(Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})=\E(U\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}) +\Var(\hat Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}), and  ar U and B are unbiased for the two terms; 2. (Total variance.)  ar Q is unbiased for \E(Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}), and T= ar U+\Bigl(1+ rac1M\Bigr)B {#eq-mis-rubin-total} is unbiased for the variance of Q- ar Q given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}; 3. (Degrees of freedom.) treating  ar U as fixed and (M-1)B/\Var(\hat Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}) as \chi^2_{M-1}, the variance of T matches that of a scaled \chi^2_{
u} variable with 
u=(M-1)\Bigl\{ rac{T}{(1+M^{-1})B}\Bigr\}^{2}= rac{M-1}{\gamma^{2}}, \qquad \gamma= rac{(1+M^{-1})B}{T}, {#eq-mis-rubin-df} and the interval  ar Q\pm t_{
u,1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{T} is the standard multiple-imputation interval; \gamma is the estimated fraction of missing information; 4. (Efficiency.) write T_\infty=\E(U)+\Var(\hat Q) for the total variance at M=\infty and \gamma_\infty=\Var(\hat Q)/T_\infty for the limiting fraction of missing information. Then the target of T exceeds T_\infty by the factor 1+\gamma_\infty/M, so using M imputations rather than infinitely many inflates the standard error by (1+\gamma_\infty/M)^{1/2}. Throughout, condition on \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}} and write \E and \Var for the conditional operators. (a) The conditional variance decomposition applied to Q with the intermediate conditioning variable \mathbf{D} gives \Var(Q)=\E\{\Var(Q\mid\mathbf{D})\}+\Var\{\E(Q\mid\mathbf{D})\}, which is \E(U)+\Var(\hat Q) by @eq-mis-complete-calibration. The completions are draws of \mathbf{D} from its conditional law, so \E ar U=\E(U), and the sample variance of independent draws is unbiased for their variance, so \E B=\Var(\hat Q). (b) \E ar Q=\E\hat Q=\E\{\E(Q\mid\mathbf{D})\}=\E Q, so  ar Q is unbiased for the posterior mean. Because the completions are conditionally independent of Q given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}, so is  ar Q, and therefore  egin{aligned} \Var(Q- ar Q)&=\Var(Q)+\Var( ar Q) = igl\{\E(U)+\Var(\hat Q) igr\}+ rac{\Var(\hat Q)}{M}\ &=\E(U)+\Bigl(1+ rac1M\Bigr)\Var(\hat Q), \end{aligned} using (a) for the first bracket and independence of the M draws for the second term. By (a) again,  ar U and (1+M^{-1})B are unbiased for the two pieces, so T is unbiased for the whole. (c) Under the stated approximation T= ar U+(1+M^{-1})B has variance (1+M^{-1})^2\Var(B), which is 2(1+M^{-1})^2\{\Var(\hat Q)\}^2/(M-1). A scaled \chi^2_
u variable with mean T has variance 2T^2/
u. Equating and replacing \Var(\hat Q) by its estimate B,  rac{2T^{2}}{
u}= rac{2(1+M^{-1})^{2}B^{2}}{M-1} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad 
u=(M-1) rac{T^{2}}{(1+M^{-1})^{2}B^{2}}, which is @eq-mis-rubin-df. (d) By (b) the target of T is \E(U)+(1+M^{-1})\Var(\hat Q)=T_\infty+ rac{\Var(\hat Q)}{M} =T_\infty\Bigl(1+ rac{\gamma_\infty}{M}\Bigr). Taking square roots gives the inflation factor. [What the proof assumes] The calibration @eq-mis-complete-calibration is a Bayesian statement: the complete-data analysis behaves as a posterior mean and variance would. Rubin’s rules are therefore exactly right when imputation and analysis come from the same joint model, and approximately right otherwise. The degrees of freedom in part (c) can exceed the complete-data degrees of freedom, absurdly, when M is large and the sample small; Barnard and Rubin (1999) give the corrected formula. The between-imputation variance B is the price of not knowing the missing values, and  ar U is the price of having a finite sample. Adding them is the whole of multiple imputation. The factor 1+1/M is the price of using finitely many imputations to measure B. How many imputations Part (d) is why the classical advice was “three to five imputations”. With \gamma_\infty=0.3 and M=5 the standard error is inflated by 1.0296, three per cent; with \gamma_\infty=0.5 the inflation is 1.0488 at M=5 and 1.0124 at M=20. The advice has shifted upward since, for two reasons the theorem does not address. The degrees of freedom @eq-mis-rubin-df grow like M-1, so a small M gives a fat-tailed reference distribution and wide intervals even when the point estimate is fine; and  ar Q is itself a Monte Carlo average, so a second analyst with a different seed gets a different number — uncomfortable when that number is a p-value near a threshold. Imputations are cheap, and the curve in Figure 41.5.1(b) is flat past M=20 for every plausible \gamma. Coverage in practice [Five analyses of one problem] Take n=200 records with two correlated covariates and y=1+0.5x_1+0.6x_2+\varepsilon, and lose x_2 with a probability depending on x_1 and y — both recorded, so MAR — leaving about 106 complete records. Over 2000 replicates, for the coefficient of x_2: Table 41.5.1. Five analyses of the same MAR problem. The estimand is  eta_2=0.6; “mean se” is the average reported standard error and “sd” the actual spread of the estimates over 2000 replicates. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- analysis bias mean se sd coverage --------------------------- -------------- -------------- -------------- -------------- complete cases -0.151 0.101 0.102 67.2\% mean imputation -0.319 0.125 0.108 25.6\% single imputation -0.010 0.082 0.138 75.9\% multiple imputation, M=5 -0.013 0.118 0.119 95.0\% multiple imputation, M=20 -0.013 0.115 0.114 94.5\% --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Each row fails in its own way. The complete cases are biased, the missingness depending on the response. Mean imputation is far worse, and its reported standard error is larger than its actual spread. A single proper imputation removes the bias but reports a standard error three fifths of the truth, and covers 75.9\% of the time. Multiple imputation with five draws gets both right, with an average estimated fraction of missing information 0.463 and median degrees of freedom 18.3. [Figure 41.5.1. (a) Coverage of nominal 95\% intervals for  eta_2 under five analyses of the same missing-at-random problem. (b) The factor by which a finite number of imputations inflates the standard error, as a function of M, for four values of the fraction of missing information.] Figure 41.5.1. (a) Coverage of nominal 95\% intervals for  eta_2 under five analyses of the same missing-at-random problem. (b) The factor by which a finite number of imputations inflates the standard error, as a function of M, for four values of the fraction of missing information. [(a) Coverage of nominal 95\% intervals for  eta_2 under five analyses of the same missing-at-random problem. (b) The factor by which a finite number of imputations inflates the standard error, as a function of M, for four values of the fraction of missing information.] (a) Coverage of nominal 95\% intervals for  eta_2 under five analyses of the same missing-at-random problem. (b) The factor by which a finite number of imputations inflates the standard error, as a function of M, for four values of the fraction of missing information. import numpy as np n = 200 rho = 0.5 beta_true = np.array([1.0, 0.5, 0.6]) # intercept, x1, x2 def make_data(rng): x1 = rng.normal(size=n) x2 = rho * x1 + np.sqrt(1 - rho**2) * rng.normal(size=n) y = beta_true[0] + beta_true[1] * x1 + beta_true[2] * x2 + rng.normal(size=n) eta = -0.3 + 0.8 * x1 + 2.0 * (y - y.mean()) # depends on observed values only: MAR seen = rng.uniform(size=n) > 1 / (1 + np.exp(-eta)) return x1, x2, y, seen def fit(X, y): """Least squares with the coefficient of x2 and its squared standard error.""" b, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(X, y, rcond=None) dof = len(y) - X.shape[1] s2 = np.sum((y - X @ b) ** 2) / dof v = s2 * np.linalg.inv(X.T @ X)[2, 2] return b[2], v, dof def impute_once(x1, x2, y, seen, rng): """One draw from the posterior predictive distribution of the missing x2.""" Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x1, y]) # imputation model: x2 on x1 and y Zo, k = Z[seen], int(seen.sum()) g, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(Zo, x2[seen], rcond=None) sse = float(np.sum((x2[seen] - Zo @ g) ** 2)) s2_star = sse / rng.chisquare(k - Z.shape[1]) # draw the variance ... cov = s2_star * np.linalg.inv(Zo.T @ Zo) g_star = rng.multivariate_normal(g, cov) # ... then the coefficients draw = Z @ g_star + np.sqrt(s2_star) * rng.normal(size=n) return np.where(seen, x2, draw) # observed values are kept def rubin(estimates, variances): """Combine M analyses: the average, the total variance and the degrees of freedom.""" M = len(estimates) q_bar, u_bar = np.mean(estimates), np.mean(variances) b = np.var(estimates, ddof=1) total = u_bar + (1 + 1 / M) * b gamma = (1 + 1 / M) * b / total # fraction of missing information nu = (M - 1) / gamma**2 return q_bar, total, nu demo_rng = np.random.default_rng(4105) x1d, x2d, yd, seend = make_data(demo_rng) # one data set, x2 missing at random qs, us = [], [] for _ in range(5): # M = 5 completions of it xi = impute_once(x1d, x2d, yd, seend, demo_rng) qi, ui, _ = fit(np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x1d, xi]), yd) qs.append(qi) us.append(ui) q_bar, total, nu = rubin(qs, us) print(f"M = 5: estimate {q_bar:.3f}, total variance {total:.4f}, " f"degrees of freedom {nu:.1f}") Chained equations handle gaps in more than one variable: ten sweeps of the two conditional regressions below recover the coefficient of x_2 when both covariates are incomplete. rho = 0.5 beta_true = np.array([1.0, 0.5, 0.6]) # intercept, x1, x2 def make_data(rng): x1 = rng.normal(size=n) x2 = rho * x1 + np.sqrt(1 - rho**2) * rng.normal(size=n) y = beta_true[0] + beta_true[1] * x1 + beta_true[2] * x2 + rng.normal(size=n) eta = -0.3 + 0.8 * x1 + 2.0 * (y - y.mean()) # depends on observed values only: MAR seen = rng.uniform(size=n) > 1 / (1 + np.exp(-eta)) return x1, x2, y, seen rng2 = np.random.default_rng(4107) x1, x2, y, seen2 = make_data(rng2) seen1 = rng2.uniform(size=n) > 0.25 # x1 now has gaps of its own x1_work = np.where(seen1, x1, x1[seen1].mean()) # a crude start x2_work = np.where(seen2, x2, x2[seen2].mean()) for sweep in range(10): # one sweep = one pass over the variables Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x2_work, y]) # x1 given the others g, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(Z[seen1], x1[seen1], rcond=None) s = np.std(x1[seen1] - Z[seen1] @ g, ddof=3) x1_work = np.where(seen1, x1, Z @ g + s * rng2.normal(size=n)) Z = np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x1_work, y]) # x2 given the others g, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(Z[seen2], x2[seen2], rcond=None) s = np.std(x2[seen2] - Z[seen2] @ g, ddof=3) x2_work = np.where(seen2, x2, Z @ g + s * rng2.normal(size=n)) b_chained, *_ = np.linalg.lstsq(np.column_stack([np.ones(n), x1_work, x2_work]), y, rcond=None) print("after chained equations:", np.round(b_chained, 3)) Congeniality Multiple imputation splits the work between two models: the imputer’s and the analyst’s. Nothing forces them to agree, and Meng (1994) showed that the consequences depend on which way they disagree. The imputation model omits something the analysis uses. If the analyst fits an interaction and the imputer imputed one of its covariates from a model without it, the imputed values carry no interaction and the analyst’s interaction is biased towards zero. This is the serious failure, and easy to commit: the imputer usually works first, for a whole file, without knowing which analyses will be run. The imputation model includes something the analysis does not. Then the imputations are richer than the analysis needs; estimates are usually fine and Rubin’s T tends to be too large, so the procedure is conservative rather than wrong. Meng calls a pair of models congenial when both are implied by a single Bayesian model, and shows that Rubin’s variance estimator is consistent under congeniality and can be biased either way without it. Two rules follow: put into the imputation model every variable the analysis will use, including the response and every transformation and interaction to be fitted; and when imputer and analyst are different people, err towards a rich imputation model. Omitting the response from the imputation model is the single commonest mistake. It looks cautious — “I do not want to build the answer into the data” — and it is exactly backwards: it imputes covariates as though they were unrelated to the response, which biases every coefficient towards zero. The response belongs in the imputation model. Exercises A. Check your understanding [A1] Set M=1 in @thm-mis-rubin and explain why the formulas break down. Which of the three quantities in @eq-mis-rubin-pieces cannot be computed, and what does that say about single imputation? With M=1 the between-imputation sum of squares has no degrees of freedom: B is 0/0. So the part of the uncertainty that comes from not knowing the missing values cannot be estimated at all from one imputation, which is exactly why a single imputation reports  ar U alone and understates the variance. [A2] From @eq-mis-rubin-df, compute 
u for M=5 and \gamma\in\{0.1,0.3,0.5\}. At which value of \gamma does the multiple-imputation reference distribution become so heavy-tailed that the interval is more than 20\% wider than a normal-based one? Equation @eq-mis-rubin-df gives 
u=(M-1)/\gamma^2, so at M=5 the three values are 4/0.01=400, 4/0.09=44.4 and 4/0.25=16. The interval is wider than the normal-based one by the ratio of the t quantile to 1.96, and a table shows that ratio passing 1.2 at about seven degrees of freedom, that is at \gamma=\sqrt{4/7.2}\approx0.75. Only a problem in which three quarters of the information is missing costs that much at M=5 — and raising M is the cheap repair. B. Practice [B1] Construct a simulation in which the analysis fits y on x_1, x_2 and their interaction, while x_2 is imputed from a model without the interaction. Measure the bias in the interaction coefficient and its coverage. Then repeat with the interaction included in the imputation model. C. Going deeper [C1] Show that when the imputation model and the analysis model are the same Bayesian model,  ar Q and T converge as M	o\infty to the exact posterior mean and variance of Q given \mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}. Deduce that multiple imputation is, in the congenial case, a Monte Carlo scheme for a posterior that could also be sampled directly — and say why one might still prefer the imputation route.

Choosing the smoothing parameter Four smoothers have appeared: the local constant and local linear estimators of Section 43.1, the penalized spline of Section 43.3 and the smoothing spline of Section 43.4. Each is linear in \y, each has one dial, and none has said how to set it. The rules of this section approximate a risk that cannot be observed: they agree when the sample is large and the errors independent, and fail in recognisably different ways when it is not. Every smoother here is a linear operator [Linear smoothers] Call \hat{\mathbf f}= S\y a linear smoother with smoother matrix  S when  S does not depend on \y. The estimators of @def-smo-kernel, @def-smo-pspline and @thm-smo-smoothing-spline are linear smoothers, with  S given by @eq-smo-equivalent, @eq-smo-smoother and @eq-smo-reinsch-fit. Suppose \E(\Y)=\mathbf f and \Cov(\Y)=\sigma^2\I. Then: 1. (Moments.) \E(\hat{\mathbf f})= S\mathbf f, \Cov(\hat{\mathbf f})=\sigma^2 S S\T, and \E
orm{\hat{\mathbf f}-\mathbf f}^2 =
orm{(\I- S)\mathbf f}^2+\sigma^2	r( S S\T) . {#eq-smo-risk} 2. (Residuals.) \E
orm{\y-\hat{\mathbf f}}^2 =
