Let \(Q\) be the estimand and suppose the complete-data analysis is calibrated in the sense that, given the complete data \(\mathbf{D}\) , it reports \(\hat Q(\mathbf{D})\) and \(U(\mathbf{D})\) with \[ \E(Q\mid\mathbf{D})=\hat Q(\mathbf{D}),\qquad \Var(Q\mid\mathbf{D})=U(\mathbf{D}). \] {#eq-mis-complete-calibration} Let \(\mathbf{D}^{(1)},\dots,\mathbf{D}^{(M)}\) be independent draws from @eq-mis-posterior-predictive , generated by the analyst and therefore conditionally independent of \(Q\) given \(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}\) ; put \(\hat Q_\ell=\hat Q(\mathbf{D}^{(\ell)})\) and \(U_\ell=U(\mathbf{D}^{(\ell)})\) , and define \[  ar Q= rac1M\sum_{\ell}\hat Q_\ell,\qquad  ar U= rac1M\sum_{\ell}U_\ell,\qquad B= rac1{M-1}\sum_{\ell}(\hat Q_\ell- ar Q)^2 . \] {#eq-mis-rubin-pieces} Then, with all expectations conditional on \(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}\) : (Decomposition.) \(\Var(Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})=\E(U\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}) +\Var(\hat Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})\) , and \( ar U\) and \(B\) are unbiased for the two terms; (Total variance.) \( ar Q\) is unbiased for \(\E(Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})\) , and \[ T= ar U+\Bigl(1+ rac1M\Bigr)B \] {#eq-mis-rubin-total} is unbiased for the variance of \(Q- ar Q\) given \(\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}}\) ; (Degrees of freedom.) treating \( ar U\) as fixed and \((M-1)B/\Var(\hat Q\mid\mathbf{D}_{\mathrm{obs}})\) as \(\chi^2_{M-1}\) , the variance of \(T\) matches that of a scaled \(\chi^2_{
u}\) variable with \[ 
u=(M-1)\Bigl\{ rac{T}{(1+M^{-1})B}\Bigr\}^{2}= rac{M-1}{\gamma^{2}}, \qquad \gamma= rac{(1+M^{-1})B}{T}, \] {#eq-mis-rubin-df} and the interval \( ar Q\pm t_{
u,1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{T}\) is the standard multiple-imputation interval; \(\gamma\) is the estimated fraction of missing information ; (Efficiency.) write \(T_\infty=\E(U)+\Var(\hat Q)\) for the total variance at \(M=\infty\) and \(\gamma_\infty=\Var(\hat Q)/T_\infty\) for the limiting fraction of missing information. Then the target of \(T\) exceeds \(T_\infty\) by the factor \(1+\gamma_\infty/M\) , so using \(M\) imputations rather than infinitely many inflates the standard error by \((1+\gamma_\infty/M)^{1/2}\) .

