Let \( S_\lambda\) be the smoother matrix, \(\hat{\mathbf f}= S_\lambda\y\) , \(\mathrm{df}=	r( S_\lambda)\) and \(s_{ii}\) the diagonal entries of \( S_\lambda\) — the leverage of @prp-proj-leverage and Section 20.2 , read now for a smoother instead of a projection. (The leave-one-out shortcut is exact.) For a penalized spline and for a smoothing spline, \[ \mathrm{CV}(\lambda)= rac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\{y_i-\hat f^{(-i)}(x_i)\}^2 = rac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\Bigl( rac{y_i-\hat f(x_i)}{1-s_{ii}}\Bigr)^2 , \] {#eq-smo-cv} where \(\hat f^{(-i)}\) is the fit computed without case \(i\) at the same \(\lambda\) . The same identity holds for a local polynomial estimator, with \(s_{ii}=\ell_i(x_i)\) . (Generalized cross-validation.) Replacing each \(s_{ii}\) by the average \(\mathrm{df}/n\) gives @def-sel-gcv , \[ \mathrm{GCV}(\lambda)= rac{n^{-1}
orm{\y-\hat{\mathbf f}}^2}{(1-\mathrm{df}/n)^2} , \] {#eq-smo-gcv} which needs only the trace, and satisfies \(\mathrm{GCV}=n^{-1}
orm{\y-\hat{\mathbf f}}^2\,(1+2\,\mathrm{df}/n)+O(\mathrm{df}^2/n^2)\) . (Information criteria.) Under normal errors with \(\sigma^2\) profiled out, @prp-sel-aic-bic gives \(\mathrm{AIC}(\lambda)=n\log(
orm{\y-\hat{\mathbf f}}^2/n)+2\,\mathrm{df}\) . Its small-sample correction (Hurvich, Simonoff and Tsai, 1998) is \[ \mathrm{AIC_c}(\lambda)=\log\Bigl( rac{
orm{\y-\hat{\mathbf f}}^2}{n}\Bigr) +1+ rac{2(\mathrm{df}+1)}{n-\mathrm{df}-2} , \] {#eq-smo-aicc} which penalizes large \(\mathrm{df}\) much more heavily and has a pole at \(\mathrm{df}=n-2\) . (What they estimate.) If \(\Cov(\Y)=\sigma^2\I\) , then \(\E\{\mathrm{CV}(\lambda)\}\) is, up to \(O(n^{-1})\) terms, the average prediction error at a fresh response, which exceeds \(n^{-1}\) times the risk @eq-smo-risk by \(\sigma^2\) . A minimizer of any of the three is therefore aiming at @eq-smo-risk , not at \(\lambda\) itself, and nothing here estimates a “true” smoothing parameter, since no such thing is assumed to exist.

